{"task_id": "Mbpp/92", "solution": "```python\ndef is_undulating(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given positive integer is an undulating number.\n    An undulating number strictly alternates between two distinct digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(number, int) or number <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    s_number = str(number)\n    n = len(s_number)\n\n    if n < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Identify the first two distinct digits for the alternating pattern.\n    digit_a = s_number[0]\n    digit_b = None\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        if s_number[i] != digit_a:\n            digit_b = s_number[i]\n            break\n    \n    # If all digits are the same (e.g., 111), it's not undulating.\n    if digit_b is None:\n        return False\n\n    # Verify that digits strictly alternate between digit_a and digit_b.\n    for i in range(n):\n        if i % 2 == 0:  # Even index, expects digit_a\n            if s_number[i] != digit_a:\n                return False\n        else:  # Odd index, expects digit_b\n            if s_number[i] != digit_b:\n                return False\n    \n    return True\n```"}
