{"task_id": "Mbpp/109", "solution": "```python\ndef odd_equivalent(binary_string: str, rotations: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of odd decimal equivalents obtained by rotating a binary string.\n    Checks for oddness by inspecting the least significant bit (rightmost character).\n    \"\"\"\n    if not all(c in '01' for c in binary_string):\n        raise ValueError(\"binary_string must contain only '0' or '1'.\")\n    if rotations < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"rotations must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    if not binary_string:\n        return 0\n\n    odd_count = 0\n    current_binary = binary_string\n    n = len(binary_string)\n\n    for _ in range(rotations):\n        # A number is odd if its binary representation ends with '1'.\n        if current_binary and current_binary[-1] == '1':\n            odd_count += 1\n\n        # Perform a single left rotation if the string is not empty.\n        if n > 0:\n            current_binary = current_binary[1:] + current_binary[0]\n        else:\n            # If string is empty, no rotation is possible; break early.\n            break\n\n    return odd_count\n```"}
