{"task_id": "Mbpp/137", "solution": "```python\ndef zero_count(numbers: list[int]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the ratio of zeroes to non-zeroes in a list of integers.\n    Handles division by zero cases by returning float('inf') or 0.0 as appropriate.\n    \"\"\"\n    num_zeroes = 0\n    num_non_zeroes = 0\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        if num == 0:\n            num_zeroes += 1\n        else:\n            num_non_zeroes += 1\n\n    if num_non_zeroes == 0:\n        if num_zeroes > 0:\n            return float('inf')\n        else:\n            return 0.0\n    else:\n        return float(num_zeroes) / num_non_zeroes\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/138", "solution": "```python\ndef is_Sum_Of_Powers_Of_Two(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if an integer can be expressed as a sum of non-zero powers of two.\n    This is true if and only if the number is a positive even integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(number, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'number' must be an integer.\")\n\n    # A sum of non-zero powers of 2 (2, 4, 8, ...) must be positive and even.\n    # Conversely, any positive even number can be formed this way.\n    return number > 0 and number % 2 == 0\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/138", "solution": "def is_Sum_Of_Powers_Of_Two(num: int) -> bool:\n    if num <= 0:\n        return False\n    # Checks if a positive number has more than one '1' bit in its binary representation.\n    # This efficiently determines if it's a sum of distinct powers of two, excluding single powers.\n    return (num & (num - 1)) != 0"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/123", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef amicable_numbers_sum(limit: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of all amicable numbers from 1 to a specified number.\n    This function acts as the main entry point to the amicable number utility.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def calculate_proper_divisor_sum(n: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Calculates the sum of proper divisors for a given positive integer.\n        Proper divisors are all divisors of n, excluding n itself.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\n        if n == 1:\n            return 0\n        \n        s = 1  # 1 is always a proper divisor for n > 1\n        # Iterate up to the square root of n for efficient divisor finding.\n        for i in range(2, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                s += i\n                if i * i != n:  # If i is not the square root, add n/i as well.\n                    s += n // i\n        return s\n\n    if not isinstance(limit, int) or limit <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Limit must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    amicable_set = set()\n    for n in range(1, limit + 1):\n        if n in amicable_set:  # Skip numbers already processed as part of a pair.\n            continue\n\n        sum_div_n = calculate_proper_divisor_sum(n)\n        # Check if sum_div_n could form a valid pair and is within the limit.\n        if sum_div_n > n and sum_div_n <= limit:\n            sum_div_sum_div_n = calculate_proper_divisor_sum(sum_div_n)\n            if sum_div_sum_div_n == n:  # If the sum of divisors of sum_div_n is n, it's an amicable pair.\n                amicable_set.add(n)\n                amicable_set.add(sum_div_n)\n    return sum(amicable_set)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/137", "solution": "```python\ndef zero_count(data: list[int]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the ratio of zeroes to non-zeroes in an array of integers.\n\n    Args:\n        data: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        The ratio as a float. Returns 0.0 for an empty array or an array\n        with no zeroes. Returns float('inf') if there are zeroes but no\n        non-zeroes.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not data:\n        return 0.0\n\n    zero_count = 0\n    non_zero_count = 0\n\n    for item in data:\n        if item == 0:\n            zero_count += 1\n        else:\n            non_zero_count += 1\n\n    if non_zero_count == 0:\n        return float('inf')\n    \n    return float(zero_count) / non_zero_count\n```"}
