{"task_id": "Mbpp/282", "solution": "```python\ndef sub_list(list1: list[int | float], list2: list[int | float]) -> list[int | float]:\n    \"\"\"Subtracts elements of list2 from corresponding elements of list1 element-wise.\n    Raises ValueError or TypeError for invalid inputs as specified.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not list1 or not list2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input lists cannot be empty.\")\n    if len(list1) != len(list2):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input lists must have the same length.\")\n\n    result = []\n    for i in range(len(list1)):\n        if not isinstance(list1[i], (int, float)) or not isinstance(list2[i], (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements in input lists must be numbers (int or float).\")\n        result.append(list1[i] - list2[i])\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/285", "solution": "import re\n\n# Compiled regular expression for efficient pattern matching.\n# Matches 'a' followed by exactly two or three 'b' characters.\n_pattern_regex = re.compile(r'a(bb|bbb)')\n\ndef text_match_two_three(text: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the input string contains 'a' followed by two or three 'b' characters.\n    Raises TypeError if the input is not a string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(text, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'text' must be a string.\")\n    return bool(_pattern_regex.search(text))"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/287", "solution": "```python\ndef square_Sum(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of squares of the first n even natural numbers.\n    Input n must be a non-negative integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Parameter 'n' must be an integer, but got \" + type(n).__name__ + \".\")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Parameter 'n' must be a non-negative integer, but got \" + str(n) + \".\")\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    # Using the mathematical formula: Sum of (2i)^2 for i=1 to n is 2*n*(n+1)*(2n+1)/3.\n    # The sum of the first n squares is n(n+1)(2n+1)/6\n    # The sum of the squares of the first n even numbers is sum((2i)^2 for i=1 to n)\n    # = sum(4*i^2 for i=1 to n)\n    # = 4 * sum(i^2 for i=1 to n)\n    # = 4 * n(n+1)(2n+1)/6\n    # = 2 * n(n+1)(2n+1)/3\n    total_sum = (2 * n * (n + 1) * (2 * n + 1)) // 3\n    return total_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/287", "solution": "```python\ndef square_Sum(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of squares of the first n even natural numbers.\n    Returns 0 if n is not positive.\"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    total_sum = 0\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        even_number = 2 * i\n        total_sum += even_number ** 2\n    return total_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/290", "solution": "```python\ndef max_length(list_of_lists: list) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the list of maximum length in a list of lists.\n\n    Args:\n        list_of_lists: A list of lists.\n\n    Returns:\n        A tuple containing the maximum length and the first list\n        that has this maximum length. Returns (0, []) for an empty\n        list of lists or a list of lists containing only empty lists.\n    \"\"\"\n    max_len = 0\n    longest_list = []\n\n    for sublist in list_of_lists:\n        if len(sublist) > max_len:\n            max_len = len(sublist)\n            longest_list = sublist\n    \n    return (max_len, longest_list)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/286", "solution": "```python\ndef max_sub_array_sum_repeated(arr: list[int], n: int, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the largest sum of a contiguous subarray in an array repeated k times.\n    Handles edge cases for k=0, k=1, and considers sums spanning multiple repetitions.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _max_subarray_sum(current_arr: list[int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Implements Kadane's algorithm to find the maximum contiguous subarray sum.\n        Handles arrays with all negative numbers by returning the largest single element.\n        \"\"\"\n        max_so_far = 0\n        current_max = 0\n        has_positive = False\n        max_single_element = -float('inf')\n\n        if not current_arr:\n            return 0\n\n        for x in current_arr:\n            if x >= 0:\n                has_positive = True\n            max_single_element = max(max_single_element, x)\n            current_max += x\n            if current_max < 0:\n                current_max = 0\n            max_so_far = max(max_so_far, current_max)\n        \n        # If all numbers are negative, max_so_far will be 0.\n        # In this case, the max subarray sum is the largest single element.\n        if not has_positive and max_so_far == 0:\n            return max_single_element\n            \n        return max_so_far\n\n    if k == 0:\n        return 0\n    \n    if k == 1:\n        return _max_subarray_sum(arr)\n    \n    # For k > 1, determine max sum by considering up to two repetitions and the total array sum.\n    total_arr_sum = sum(arr)\n    \n    # Max sum within a single repetition.\n    max_sum_single_arr = _max_subarray_sum(arr)\n    \n    # Max sum spanning across up to two repetitions.\n    doubled_arr = arr + arr\n    max_sum_doubled_arr = _max_subarray_sum(doubled_arr)\n    \n    if total_arr_sum > 0:\n        # If the total array sum is positive, the best sum can include (k-2) full arrays.\n        # This is combined with the best sum found in up to two repetitions.\n        # We take max with max_sum_single_arr as well, in case k-2 is negative (i.e. k=2)\n        # or if the best sum is still within a single repetition.\n        return max(max_sum_single_arr, max_sum_doubled_arr, total_arr_sum * (k - 2) + max_sum_doubled_arr)\n    else:\n        # If the total array sum is non-positive, adding more repetitions won't increase the sum.\n        # Thus, the max sum is found within at most two repetitions.\n        return max(max_sum_single_arr, max_sum_doubled_arr)\n\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/290", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Any, List, Tuple\n\ndef max_length(list_of_lists: List[List[Any]]) -> Tuple[int, List[Any]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies the longest sublist and its length within a collection of lists.\n    Returns (0, []) for an empty input list.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not list_of_lists:\n        return 0, []\n\n    max_length_found = 0\n    longest_list: List[Any] = []\n\n    for sublist in list_of_lists:\n        if not isinstance(sublist, list):\n            raise TypeError(\"Input list_of_lists must contain only lists.\")\n        \n        current_length = len(sublist)\n        if current_length >= max_length_found:\n            max_length_found = current_length\n            longest_list = sublist\n            \n    return max_length_found, longest_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/286", "solution": "import math\n\ndef _kadane(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Implements Kadane's algorithm to find the maximum contiguous subarray sum.\n    Handles arrays with all negative numbers by returning the largest element.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return 0\n\n    max_so_far = -math.inf\n    current_max = 0\n\n    for x in arr:\n        current_max += x\n        if current_max > max_so_far:\n            max_so_far = current_max\n        if current_max < 0:\n            current_max = 0\n    \n    # Handle case where all numbers are negative.\n    # If the standard Kadane's algorithm yields 0 (e.g., [ -1, 0, -2 ]), but there are negative numbers,\n    # it implies no positive sum was found, and the maximum could be a single largest negative element.\n    if max_so_far == 0 and any(x < 0 for x in arr):\n        return max(arr)\n    \n    return max_so_far if max_so_far != -math.inf else 0\n\n\ndef max_sub_array_sum_repeated(arr: list[int], arr_len: int, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the largest sum of a contiguous subarray in the modified array\n    which is formed by repeating the given array k times.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return 0\n    \n    if k == 1:\n        return _kadane(arr)\n\n    total_sum = sum(arr)\n    \n    # Apply Kadane's algorithm to two concatenated arrays to find max sum spanning one or two blocks.\n    arr_double = arr + arr\n    max_sum_two_arrays = _kadane(arr_double)\n\n    if total_sum > 0:\n        # If the total sum of the array is positive, additional repetitions contribute positively.\n        # The result is max sum within two arrays plus (k-2) full array sums.\n        return max_sum_two_arrays + (k - 2) * total_sum\n    else:\n        # If total sum is non-positive, repeating more than twice won't increase the maximum sum.\n        # The maximum sum will be found within the first two logically repeated arrays.\n        return max_sum_two_arrays"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/292", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef find(dividend: float | int, divisor: float | int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the integer quotient of two numbers, rounded down.\n    Raises TypeError if inputs are non-numeric, or ZeroDivisionError if divisor is zero.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(dividend, (int, float)) or not isinstance(divisor, (int, float)):\n        raise TypeError(\"Both dividend and divisor must be numeric.\")\n    if divisor == 0:\n        raise ZeroDivisionError(\"Divisor cannot be zero.\")\n    return math.floor(dividend / divisor)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/292", "solution": "```python\nclass DivisionByZeroError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for division by zero errors.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message=\"Cannot divide by zero\"):\n        self.message = message\n        super().__init__(self.message)\n\ndef find(numerator: int, denominator: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the integer quotient of two numbers, rounded down to the nearest integer.\n\n    Args:\n        numerator: The dividend.\n        denominator: The divisor.\n\n    Returns:\n        The integer quotient.\n\n    Raises:\n        DivisionByZeroError: If the denominator is zero.\n    \"\"\"\n    if denominator == 0:\n        raise DivisionByZeroError(\"Cannot divide by zero\")\n    return numerator // denominator\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/294", "solution": "```python\ndef max_val(data_list: list) -> int | float | None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum numeric value (int or float) in a list.\n    Returns None if the list is empty or contains no numeric values.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not data_list:\n        return None\n\n    max_numeric_value = None\n\n    for item in data_list:\n        if isinstance(item, (int, float)):\n            if max_numeric_value is None or item > max_numeric_value:\n                max_numeric_value = item\n    \n    return max_numeric_value\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/293", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\nclass InvalidInputError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input values in triangle calculations.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\nERROR_MESSAGE_NON_POSITIVE = \"Side lengths must be positive numbers.\"\n\ndef otherside_rightangle(side1: float, side2: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle using the Pythagorean theorem.\n    Raises InvalidInputError if side lengths are not positive numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if side1 <= 0 or side2 <= 0:\n        raise InvalidInputError(ERROR_MESSAGE_NON_POSITIVE)\n    \n    hypotenuse_squared = side1**2 + side2**2\n    return math.sqrt(hypotenuse_squared)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/294", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Optional, Union\n\ndef max_val(data_list: List[any]) -> Optional[Union[int, float]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum numeric value in a heterogeneous list.\n    It ignores non-numeric elements and returns None if no numeric values are found.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(data_list, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n\n    max_value: Optional[Union[int, float]] = None\n\n    for item in data_list:\n        if isinstance(item, (int, float)):\n            if max_value is None or item > max_value:\n                max_value = item\n    return max_value\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/293", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\nclass InvalidSideError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid triangle side inputs.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        super().__init__(message)\n\ndef otherside_rightangle(side1: float, side2: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the length of the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle.\n    Requires two positive numerical values representing the lengths of the perpendicular sides.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(side1, (int, float)) or side1 <= 0:\n        raise InvalidSideError(\"Side 1 must be a positive number.\")\n    if not isinstance(side2, (int, float)) or side2 <= 0:\n        raise InvalidSideError(\"Side 2 must be a positive number.\")\n\n    return math.sqrt(side1**2 + side2**2)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/297", "solution": "```python\ndef flatten_list(nested_list: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Flattens a given nested list structure into a single-level list.\n    It raises a TypeError if the input is not a list.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(nested_list, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n\n    flattened = []\n    for element in nested_list:\n        if isinstance(element, list):\n            flattened.extend(flatten_list(element))\n        else:\n            flattened.append(element)\n    return flattened\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/297", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Union\n\ndef flatten_list(nested_list: List[Union[int, List]]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Flattens a given nested list containing integers and other lists.\n    Recursively processes sub-lists to extract all integer elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    flattened = []\n    for element in nested_list:\n        if isinstance(element, int):\n            flattened.append(element)\n        elif isinstance(element, list):\n            flattened.extend(flatten_list(element))\n    return flattened\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/296", "solution": "```python\ndef get_Inv_Count(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the total number of inversions in an array using a merge sort-based approach.\"\"\"\n    # Validate input type and elements as specified in FR1.\n    if not isinstance(arr, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n    for item in arr:\n        if not isinstance(item, int):\n            raise ValueError(\"All elements in the list must be integers.\")\n\n    n = len(arr)\n    # A temporary array is used for efficient merging, avoiding repeated memory allocations.\n    temp_arr = [0] * n\n\n    def _merge_and_count(arr_local: list[int], temp_arr_local: list[int], left: int, mid: int, right: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Merges two sorted subarrays and counts inversions formed during the merge step.\"\"\"\n        i = left\n        j = mid + 1\n        k = left\n        inv_count = 0\n\n        while i <= mid and j <= right:\n            if arr_local[i] <= arr_local[j]:\n                temp_arr_local[k] = arr_local[i]\n                i += 1\n            else:\n                # An inversion occurs if arr_local[i] > arr_local[j]. All remaining elements in the left subarray (from i to mid) are also greater than arr_local[j].\n                temp_arr_local[k] = arr_local[j]\n                inv_count += (mid - i + 1)\n                j += 1\n            k += 1\n\n        # Copy any remaining elements from the left subarray.\n        while i <= mid:\n            temp_arr_local[k] = arr_local[i]\n            i += 1\n            k += 1\n\n        # Copy any remaining elements from the right subarray.\n        while j <= right:\n            temp_arr_local[k] = arr_local[j]\n            j += 1\n            k += 1\n\n        # Copy the merged elements back to the original array segment.\n        for loop_var in range(left, right + 1):\n            arr_local[loop_var] = temp_arr_local[loop_var]\n\n        return inv_count\n\n    def _merge_sort_and_count(arr_local: list[int], temp_arr_local: list[int], left: int, right: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Recursively divides the array into halves, sorts them, and sums inversions.\"\"\"\n        inv_count = 0\n        if left < right:\n            mid = (left + right) // 2\n            # Count inversions in the left half, then the right half.\n            inv_count += _merge_sort_and_count(arr_local, temp_arr_local, left, mid)\n            inv_count += _merge_sort_and_count(arr_local, temp_arr_local, mid + 1, right)\n            # Count inversions formed when merging the two halves.\n            inv_count += _merge_and_count(arr_local, temp_arr_local, left, mid, right)\n        return inv_count\n\n    # Begin the recursive merge sort and inversion counting process.\n    return _merge_sort_and_count(arr, temp_arr, 0, n - 1)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/296", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\nclass InvalidInputError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input to inversion counting functions.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        super().__init__(message)\n        self.message = message\n\nclass InputValidator:\n    \"\"\"Provides methods to validate input arrays.\"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def validate_array(arr: list) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates if the input is a list and contains only integers.\n        Raises InvalidInputError on failure.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(arr, list):\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n        for item in arr:\n            if not isinstance(item, int):\n                raise InvalidInputError(\"All elements in the list must be integers.\")\n\nclass InversionCounter:\n    \"\"\"\n    A utility class to count inversions in an array.\n    Uses a merge sort-based algorithm for O(n log n) efficiency.\n    \"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def get_Inv_Count(arr: List[int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Calculates the total number of inversions in the given array.\n        Validates the input and then uses a recursive merge sort approach.\n        \"\"\"\n        InputValidator.validate_array(arr)\n        _, count = InversionCounter._merge_sort_and_count(list(arr)) # Pass a copy to avoid modifying original\n        return count\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _merge_sort_and_count(arr: List[int]) -> Tuple[List[int], int]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Recursively divides the array, sorts sub-arrays, and counts inversions during merge.\n        This is the core O(n log n) algorithm.\n        \"\"\"\n        n = len(arr)\n        if n <= 1:\n            return arr, 0\n\n        mid = n // 2\n        left_half, left_inversions = InversionCounter._merge_sort_and_count(arr[:mid])\n        right_half, right_inversions = InversionCounter._merge_sort_and_count(arr[mid:])\n\n        merged_arr, merge_inversions = InversionCounter._merge_and_count(left_half, right_half)\n\n        return merged_arr, left_inversions + right_inversions + merge_inversions\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _merge_and_count(left: List[int], right: List[int]) -> Tuple[List[int], int]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Merges two sorted sub-arrays and counts inversions between them.\n        Inversions occur when an element from the right array is picked before a larger element from the left array.\n        \"\"\"\n        merged = []\n        inversions = 0\n        i, j = 0, 0\n\n        while i < len(left) and j < len(right):\n            if left[i] <= right[j]:\n                merged.append(left[i])\n                i += 1\n            else:\n                merged.append(right[j])\n                # All remaining elements in left_half are greater than right_half[j]\n                inversions += (len(left) - i)\n                j += 1\n\n        merged.extend(left[i:])\n        merged.extend(right[j:])\n\n        return merged, inversions\n\nget_Inv_Count = InversionCounter.get_Inv_Count\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/300", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef count_binary_seq(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the count of all binary sequences of length 2n where the sum\n    of the first n bits equals the sum of the last n bits (C(2n, n)).\n    Raises TypeError if n is not an integer or ValueError if n is negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    return math.comb(2 * n, n)\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/299", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nfrom typing import List, Tuple, Optional\n\ndef max_aggregate(data: List[Tuple[str, int]]) -> Tuple[Optional[str], int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum aggregate score from a list of name-score tuples.\n    Returns the name with the highest total score and their score, or (None, 0) if input is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(data, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'data' must be a list.\")\n    \n    if not data:\n        return (None, 0)\n\n    aggregates = defaultdict(int)\n    for entry in data:\n        # Validate each entry in the list\n        if not isinstance(entry, tuple) or len(entry) != 2:\n            raise TypeError(\"Each entry in data must be a tuple of (str, int).\")\n        \n        name, score = entry\n        if not isinstance(name, str) or not isinstance(score, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"Each entry in data must be a tuple of (str, int).\")\n        \n        aggregates[name] += score\n\n    max_name: Optional[str] = None\n    max_score: int = 0\n\n    # Find the name with the maximum aggregate score.\n    # The 'if aggregates:' check ensures max() is called on a non-empty dictionary.\n    if aggregates:\n        max_name = max(aggregates, key=aggregates.get) # type: ignore\n        max_score = aggregates[max_name] \n\n    return (max_name, max_score)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/299", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple, Dict\n\ndef max_aggregate(data: List[Tuple[str, int]]) -> Tuple[str, int]:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the total score for each unique name and returns the name with the highest total score.\n    Returns (None, 0) if the input list is empty.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(data, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'data' must be a list.\")\n\n    aggregated_scores: Dict[str, int] = {}\n\n    for entry in data:\n        if not isinstance(entry, tuple) or len(entry) != 2:\n            raise ValueError(\"Each entry in 'data' must be a tuple of (name: str, score: int).\")\n        name, score = entry\n        if not isinstance(name, str) or not isinstance(score, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"Each entry in 'data' must be a tuple of (name: str, score: int).\")\n\n        aggregated_scores[name] = aggregated_scores.get(name, 0) + score\n\n    if not aggregated_scores:\n        return (None, 0)\n\n    max_name: str = \"\"\n    max_score: int = -1\n\n    for name, score in aggregated_scores.items():\n        if score > max_score:\n            max_score = score\n            max_name = name\n\n    return (max_name, max_score)\n```\n"}
