{"task_id": "Mbpp/414", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections.abc import Iterable, Sized\n\ndef overlapping(seq1, seq2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if any element in the first sequence exists in the second sequence.\n    Raises TypeError if either seq1 or seq2 is not an iterable or not sized.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Validate inputs are both iterable and sized, as required for optimizations.\n    if not (isinstance(seq1, Iterable) and isinstance(seq1, Sized)):\n        raise TypeError(\"seq1 must be a sized iterable (e.g., list, tuple, set, str).\")\n    if not (isinstance(seq2, Iterable) and isinstance(seq2, Sized)):\n        raise TypeError(\"seq2 must be a sized iterable (e.g., list, tuple, set, str).\")\n\n    # Convert the smaller sequence to a set for efficient O(1) average-case lookups.\n    if len(seq1) <= len(seq2):\n        set1 = set(seq1)\n        for item in seq2:\n            if item in set1:\n                return True\n    else:\n        set2 = set(seq2)\n        for item in seq1:\n            if item in set2:\n                return True\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/415", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\ndef max_Product(numbers: List[int]) -> Tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the pair of integers with the highest product from the given list.\n    Handles various cases including positive, negative, and mixed numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(numbers, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list must contain at least two numbers.\")\n    for num in numbers:\n        if not isinstance(num, int):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements in the list must be integers.\")\n\n    # Sorting allows efficient identification of the two largest and two smallest elements (O(N log N)).\n    # The highest product will be from either the two largest numbers or the two smallest (most negative).\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n    n = len(sorted_numbers)\n\n    # Calculate product of the two largest numbers.\n    prod_largest = sorted_numbers[n-1] * sorted_numbers[n-2]\n    pair_largest = (sorted_numbers[n-1], sorted_numbers[n-2])\n\n    # Calculate product of the two smallest (most negative) numbers.\n    prod_smallest = sorted_numbers[0] * sorted_numbers[1]\n    pair_smallest = (sorted_numbers[0], sorted_numbers[1])\n\n    # Compare the two candidate products and return the pair with the highest.\n    if prod_largest >= prod_smallest:\n        return tuple(sorted(pair_largest))\n    else:\n        return tuple(sorted(pair_smallest))\n```"}
