{"task_id": "Mbpp/581", "solution": "```python\ndef surface_Area(base_edge: float, height: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total surface area of a square pyramid.\n    The 'height' parameter is interpreted as the slant height, based on the assertion surface_Area(3,4) == 33.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(base_edge, (int, float)) or base_edge <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Base edge must be a positive number.\")\n    if not isinstance(height, (int, float)) or height <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Height must be a positive number.\")\n\n    base_edge = float(base_edge)\n    height = float(height)\n\n    base_area = base_edge * base_edge\n    lateral_surface_area = 2 * base_edge * height\n    \n    return base_area + lateral_surface_area\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/585", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Union\n\ndef expensive_items(items: List[Dict[str, Union[str, float, int]]], n: int) -> List[Dict[str, Union[str, float]]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the n most expensive items from a list of item dictionaries, sorted by price descending.\n    Performs comprehensive validation for inputs `items` and `n`.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(items, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'items' must be a list.\")\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n\n    if n <= 0:\n        return []\n    if not items:\n        return []\n\n    # Validate each item dictionary and ensure price is a float.\n    processed_items: List[Dict[str, Union[str, float]]] = []\n    for item_data in items:\n        if not isinstance(item_data, dict):\n            raise ValueError(\"Each item in 'items' must be a dictionary.\")\n        if 'name' not in item_data or 'price' not in item_data:\n            raise ValueError(\"Each item dictionary must contain 'name' and 'price' keys.\")\n        if not isinstance(item_data['name'], str):\n            raise ValueError(\"Item 'name' must be a string.\")\n        \n        price_val = item_data['price']\n        if not isinstance(price_val, (int, float)):\n            raise ValueError(\"Item 'price' must be a numeric value.\")\n        \n        processed_items.append({'name': item_data['name'], 'price': float(price_val)})\n\n    # Sort items by price in descending order.\n    # This is an efficient O(N log N) operation for N items.\n    sorted_items = sorted(processed_items, key=lambda item: item['price'], reverse=True)\n\n    # Return the top n items. If n exceeds the list size, all items are returned.\n    return sorted_items[:n]\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/585", "solution": "```python\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Any\n\n@dataclass\nclass Item:\n    \"\"\"Represents an item with a name and a price.\n    Acts as a data transfer object (DTO).\"\"\"\n    name: str\n    price: float\n\n    def to_dict(self) -> dict:\n        \"\"\"Converts the Item object to a dictionary.\"\"\"\n        return {'name': self.name, 'price': self.price}\n\ndef expensive_items(items: List[Dict[str, Any]], n: int) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the n most expensive items from a given dataset.\n    Handles various input validations and edge cases.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(items, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'items' must be a list.\")\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n\n    if n <= 0:\n        return []\n\n    valid_items: List[Item] = []\n    for item_dict in items:\n        if not isinstance(item_dict, dict):\n            continue\n\n        name = item_dict.get('name')\n        price = item_dict.get('price')\n\n        # Skip items missing name or price, or if name is not a string\n        if not isinstance(name, str) or price is None:\n            continue\n\n        try:\n            price_float = float(price)\n            valid_items.append(Item(name=name, price=price_float))\n        except (ValueError, TypeError):\n            # Skip items with non-numeric prices\n            continue\n\n    # Sort items by price in descending order\n    valid_items.sort(key=lambda item: item.price, reverse=True)\n\n    # Get the top n items or all available if n is too large\n    top_n_items = valid_items[:n]\n\n    # Convert back to list of dictionaries for output\n    return [item.to_dict() for item in top_n_items]\n```"}
