{"task_id": "Mbpp/589", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef perfect_squares(num1: int, num2: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds all perfect squares within the inclusive range [num1, num2].\n    Returns a list of perfect squares in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    if num1 > num2:\n        return []\n\n    squares = []\n    # Calculate the smallest integer whose square is >= num1.\n    start_sqrt = math.ceil(math.sqrt(max(0, num1)))\n    \n    # Iterate and add squares until they exceed num2.\n    i = start_sqrt\n    while True:\n        square = i * i\n        if square > num2:\n            break\n        if square >= num1:\n            squares.append(square)\n        i += 1\n    \n    return squares\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/589", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef perfect_squares(start: int, end: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies and returns all perfect squares within the inclusive range [start, end].\n    Raises TypeError for non-integer inputs or ValueError if start > end.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(start, int) or not isinstance(end, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Start and end must be integers.\")\n    if start > end:\n        raise ValueError(\"Start cannot be greater than end.\")\n\n    perfect_squares_list = []\n    # Ensure iteration starts from 0 if 'start' is negative, as 0 is the smallest perfect square.\n    actual_start = max(0, start) \n\n    for num in range(actual_start, end + 1):\n        if num == 0:\n            perfect_squares_list.append(num)\n            continue\n        \n        # Check if num is a perfect square using integer square root.\n        sqrt_num = math.isqrt(num)\n        if sqrt_num * sqrt_num == num:\n            perfect_squares_list.append(num)\n    \n    return perfect_squares_list\n```"}
