{"task_id": "Mbpp/605", "solution": "import math\n\ndef prime_num(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if an integer is a prime number.\n    It efficiently determines primality using trial division.\"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        return False\n    if n == 2:\n        return True\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n\n    # Check for odd divisors from 3 up to the square root of n.\n    for i in range(3, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1, 2):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/603", "solution": "```python\ndef get_ludic(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates and returns a list of \"lucid numbers\" up to n.\n    Lucid numbers are defined as 1 and all prime numbers up to n.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n    if n == 0:\n        return []\n\n    lucid_numbers = []\n    if n >= 1:\n        lucid_numbers.append(1)\n        \n    # If n is 0 or 1, we're done (1 is already added if n=1)\n    if n < 2:\n        return lucid_numbers\n\n    # Sieve of Eratosthenes to find prime numbers up to n efficiently.\n    is_prime = [True] * (n + 1)\n    is_prime[0] = is_prime[1] = False\n\n    for p in range(2, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n        if is_prime[p]:\n            for multiple in range(p * p, n + 1, p):\n                is_prime[multiple] = False\n\n    for p in range(2, n + 1):\n        if is_prime[p]:\n            lucid_numbers.append(p)\n\n    return lucid_numbers\n```"}
