{"task_id": "Mbpp/752", "solution": "```python\nclass InvalidInputError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input to Jacobsthal functions.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\ndef validate_non_negative_integer(n: object) -> None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates if the given input n is a non-negative integer.\n    Raises InvalidInputError if n is not an integer or is negative.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n_jacobsthal_memo = {0: 0, 1: 1}\n\ndef jacobsthal_num(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the nth Jacobsthal number using the recurrence relation.\n    The Jacobsthal sequence starts with 0, 1, 1, 3, 5, 11, ...\n    J(n) = J(n-1) + 2 * J(n-2) for n > 1.\n\n    Args:\n        n: The index of the Jacobsthal number to compute (non-negative integer).\n\n    Returns:\n        The nth Jacobsthal number.\n\n    Raises:\n        InvalidInputError: If n is not a non-negative integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    validate_non_negative_integer(n)\n\n    if n in _jacobsthal_memo:\n        return _jacobsthal_memo[n]\n\n    # Calculate iteratively to avoid recursion depth limits and stack overflow\n    # for very large n, while still leveraging memoization.\n    # The memoization dictionary already handles base cases 0 and 1.\n    for i in range(2, n + 1):\n        if i not in _jacobsthal_memo:\n            _jacobsthal_memo[i] = _jacobsthal_memo[i-1] + 2 * _jacobsthal_memo[i-2]\n    \n    return _jacobsthal_memo[n]\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/754", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Any\n\ndef extract_index_list(l1: List[Any], l2: List[Any], l3: List[Any]) -> List[Any]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds elements that appear in all three lists at the same index.\n    Raises TypeError if any of the inputs are not lists.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not all(isinstance(arg, list) for arg in [l1, l2, l3]):\n        raise TypeError(\"All inputs must be lists.\")\n\n    common_elements = []\n    min_length = min(len(l1), len(l2), len(l3))\n\n    for i in range(min_length):\n        if l1[i] == l2[i] and l2[i] == l3[i]:\n            common_elements.append(l1[i])\n\n    return common_elements\n```"}
