{"task_id": "Mbpp/753", "solution": "```python\nimport heapq\nfrom typing import List, Tuple, Any, NamedTuple\n\nclass Record(NamedTuple):\n    \"\"\"Represents a record with a name (str) and a number (int).\n    It allows comparison based on the 'number' attribute for heap operations.\"\"\"\n    name: str\n    number: int\n\n    def __lt__(self, other: 'Record') -> bool:\n        return self.number < other.number\n\nclass InvalidInputError(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input parameters.\n    It indicates that input values do not meet specific business rules.\"\"\"\n    pass\n\ndef _validate_input_list(data: Any) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Validates if data is a list of (str, int) tuples.\n    Raises TypeError or ValueError if the input is not well-formed.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(data, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'data' must be a list.\")\n    if not data:\n        return\n\n    for i, item in enumerate(data):\n        if not isinstance(item, tuple):\n            raise TypeError(f\"Element at index {i} in 'data' is not a tuple: {item}\")\n        if len(item) != 2:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Tuple at index {i} in 'data' must have exactly two elements: {item}\")\n        if not isinstance(item[0], str):\n            raise ValueError(f\"First element of tuple at index {i} must be a string: {item[0]}\")\n        if not isinstance(item[1], int):\n            raise ValueError(f\"Second element of tuple at index {i} must be an integer: {item[1]}\")\n\ndef _validate_k_value(k: Any, data_length: int) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Validates k as a positive integer within data_length bounds.\n    Raises TypeError or InvalidInputError if k does not meet the criteria.\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(k, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'k' must be an integer.\")\n    if k <= 0:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Input 'k' must be a positive integer.\")\n    if k > data_length:\n        raise InvalidInputError(f\"Input 'k' ({k}) cannot be greater than the number of records ({data_length}).\")\n\ndef min_k(data: List[Tuple[str, int]], k: int) -> List[Tuple[str, int]]:\n    \"\"\"Finds the 'k' records with the minimum numbers from the input list.\n    Returns them sorted by number in ascending order.\"\"\"\n    _validate_input_list(data)\n    _validate_k_value(k, len(data))\n\n    record_data = [Record(name, number) for name, number in data]\n    result = heapq.nsmallest(k, record_data)\n\n    # Convert Record objects back to tuples for the return value\n    return [(record.name, record.number) for record in result]\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/755", "solution": "```python\ndef second_smallest(numbers: list[int | float]) -> int | float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the second smallest number in a list of numbers, considering duplicates.\n    Raises TypeError if input is not a list, or ValueError if the list has fewer than two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(numbers, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        raise ValueError(\"List must contain at least two elements to find the second smallest.\")\n\n    # Sorting handles duplicates and various number types (int, float, positive, negative, zero)\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n    return sorted_numbers[1]\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/758", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\nfrom typing import Any, List, Dict, Tuple\n\ndef unique_sublists(input_list: List[List[Any]]) -> Dict[Tuple[Any, ...], int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts occurrences of sublists in a list, converting each to a tuple.\n    Returns a dictionary mapping unique sublist tuples to their counts.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(input_list, list):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n\n    tuple_list = []\n    for sublist in input_list:\n        if not isinstance(sublist, list):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements in the input list must be lists.\")\n        try:\n            tuple_list.append(tuple(sublist))\n        except TypeError as e:\n            raise TypeError(f\"Sublist contains unhashable elements: {sublist}\") from e\n\n    return dict(Counter(tuple_list))\n```"}
